Analysis
Getting there took two changes to the force calculation, which is where the time goes:

Cell lists. An atom only feels neighbours within 2.5 σ but the all-pairs loop still visits every atom to find them. Instead divide the box into cells 2.5 σ wide, sort the atoms into cells, and look only in an atom’s own cell and the eight around it. That is about 50 candidates instead of 19,000 so the work grows with the number of atoms, N, instead of N^2. Building the sort on a GPU takes four small passes: count the atoms in each cell with atomicAdd, turn the counts into start offsets with a prefix sum and scatter each atom’s index into its slot.
Tiling in workgroup memory. In the all-pairs loop every thread in a workgroup reads the same neighbour’s position from slow memory, one atom at a time. A tiled loop has the 64 threads each fetch one position into fast var&lt;workgroup&gt; memory, wait at a barrier and then all read the whole tile from there. It is the standard trick for an N-body loop and it is in the benchmark below.

Press Benchmark to time all three kernels on your GPU at several sizes (it pauses the simulation while it runs). Moving a slider does not restart anything.

