Building on Compute Shader, the particles now push and pull on each other. Every pair interacts through the Lennard-Jones potential,
V(r) = 4\varepsilon\left[\left(\frac{\sigma}{r}\right)^{12} - \left(\frac{\sigma}{r}\right)^{6}\right],
which is strongly repulsive up close and weakly attractive further out. A first compute pass sums the force on each particle from every other one, and a second integrates the motion (leapfrog, which follows the same trajectory as velocity Verlet). The walls are replaced by periodic boundaries: leave on one side, re-enter on the other.
Condensing lowers the potential energy and with nothing else to take it, that energy becomes motion so on its own the box would just warm up. A Langevin thermostat takes it away: every step each particle is slightly slowed and given a small random kick, which holds the temperature at the slider’s value. The kicks need random numbers on the GPU, so each particle carries its own generator state. Particles are coloured by speed. Hot and they stay a gas. Cool them and the attraction wins and droplets of liquid condense out of it.
