How long a transform takes, measured in this browser. The red and grey lines are a line of numbers on the CPU. By the definition the time grows as the square of the length: twice as many numbers take four times as long. The fast method takes barely more than twice as long. At two thousand numbers it is already a couple of hundred times quicker, which is about what the step counts predict: 2,048 steps for each of 2,048 results against about 11. The difference keeps widening: for a million numbers it would be about fifty thousand to one.
The blue line is the GPU, which transforms a whole picture at once. A 512 by 512 picture is 262,144 numbers, 128 times as many as a line of two thousand. Compare its time with the CPU’s for that line: it is nowhere near 128 times longer. At the small sizes the blue line sits above the others, since getting the work started costs more than the work.

2026-10-13
Building on Jiggling Atoms and on the measuring tools of Is Energy Conserved?, the box starts as a triangular crystal that has been stretched out to a density of 0.5, far thinner than a crystal wants to be. It cannot hold together as one piece and soon breaks into crystalline islands floating in vapour. We look at how the atoms are arranged, in two ways.
Each atom is coloured by how hexagonal its neighbourhood is. For every bond to a neighbour closer than 1.4 σ, take the bond’s angle \theta and average e^{6i\theta} over the bonds. In a triangular crystal all six bonds line up and the magnitude, |\psi_6|, is close to 1. In a liquid the angles are scrambled and it falls towards 0. Atoms bright yellow are crystalline, blue are not. (The same idea is behind the hexatic phase.) Switch the colouring to speed to see the same atoms the way the earlier posts drew them.
At the starting temperature the islands stay crystalline with a thin vapour between them. Warm it and the islands melt into liquid droplets: the peaks wash out and the yellow drains away. Cool it and the liquid freezes into crystallites, usually with defects and grain boundaries rather than a perfect lattice.
Temperature alone does not tell the whole story, because the phase also depends on density. The density slider expands or compresses the box, and the atoms with it. At the crystal’s own density, 0.87, there is no room for a gas: even a hot box is just a dense liquid. Spread the atoms thinly and keep it warm and the colours go blue, the first peak shrinks and g(r) flattens towards 1.
